# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (August 2026), NVIDIA's NVFP4 build: 125B with 6B active,
# plus a 51B n-gram embedding table and a 4B draft head. Qwen calls it a
# preview of the Qwen4 architecture, built for long context: sparse attention,
# 12 of 48 layers attending globally. The strongest coder that can run here
# (62.5 SWE-bench Pro, 58.7 DeepSWE on Qwen's card). 256k natively, 1M with
# the YaRN setting Qwen documents.
#
# The checkpoint is 133 GB, more than this machine holds, so the stock vLLM
# image cannot serve it. spark/flash-next.sh runs it through the
# qwen3.8-Flash-DGX recipe (Apache-2.0, pinned to a commit), which patches
# vLLM 0.31 to read the 48 GiB n-gram table from the NVMe instead of keeping
# it in memory. About 75 GiB stays resident.
#
# VLLM_LONG=0 serves 500k and VLLM_LONG=1 the largest window that fits, both
# with the FP8 cache. On this machine that largest window is about 558k, not
# the 1M the model can take: the weights leave 15 GiB for the cache at the
# recipe's safe memory setting. The launcher works the number out from vLLM's
# own estimate; see the header of spark/flash-next.sh.
TITLE="Qwen3.8-Flash-Next"
DETAIL="125B, 6B active, NVFP4, 133 GB"
HF_REPO=nvidia/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-NVFP4
SERVED_NAME=qwen3.8-flash-next
MAX_LEN=500000
LONG_MAX_LEN=558000
ROPE_ORIG=262144
TOOL_PARSER=qwen3_coder
REASONING_PARSER=qwen3
KV_DTYPE=fp8_e4m3
MEM_UTIL=0.80
MIN_FREE_GB=100
RUNNER=spark/flash-next.sh
